pathprobe viewer=0 status=206 clen=Some(33554432) crange=Some("bytes 0-33554431/1073741824") bytes=33554432 secs=31.01 rate=8.7 Mbit/s ttfb=189.8ms gaps: n=17957 p50=0.1 p99=82.5 max=98.2ms stalls>500ms=0 >1s=0 >2s=0 worst_1s=7.9 Mbit/s verify=off

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS IS A PROBE, NOT THE BAR. Two reasons it does not close the NO-GO:
#
# 1. n=1 at 31 s. The bar is >=3 runs of 600 s reported as a distribution
#    (ARCH 18.5 — a single-run number is not a result).
#
# 2. AN UNEXCLUDED CONFOUND, which matters more. The SENDER is a laptop
#    tethered to a phone. Cellular uplink is typically 5-20 Mbit/s, so 8.7
#    may be the phone's upstream rather than the n0 relay. The bar's intent
#    is to characterise THE RELAY as the floor; this configuration cannot
#    separate the two.
#
#    The contaminated run reported 7.8 Mbit/s and this one 8.7. They agree —
#    but agreement between two numbers that may share a confound is not
#    corroboration, and 7.8 already sat inside the prior WORK_PLANE.md writes
#    down. That is the trap note 04b80710 named: a number that agrees with
#    your prior is the one nobody checks.
#
# TO DISAMBIGUATE, cheapest first:
#   - measure the phone's raw uplink (any speed test on the tethered Mac);
#     if it is ~9 Mbit/s, this reading is the uplink and says nothing about
#     the relay.
#   - or re-run with the ROLES REVERSED (RuggedFox serves on wired/home,
#     the Mac pulls) so the sender is not the cellular leg.
#   - or re-run relayed between two machines that both have real uplinks,
#     forcing relay by blocking direct rather than by NAT topology.
